<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ozurgeti</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Makharadze</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ozurgeti is a town in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, the administrative centre of the Guria region, a short distance inland from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast. It served as the residence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-gurieli">Gurieli</a> princes of Guria and, after the Russian annexation of 1829, as the seat of a district. Named Makharadze in the Soviet period from 1934 to 1989, it is surrounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> and citrus plantations.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ozurgeti.jpg" alt="Ozurgeti" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: გრიგოლ · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ozurgeti_in_winter.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ozurgeti lies about 200 metres above sea level, mostly between the Bzhuzhi and Natanebi rivers and bounded by hills to the north and south. The Natanebi is a subterranean river, with a good deal of the town built on top of it, while the Bzhuzhi flows above ground. The town sits on a slope levelled into three broad terraces: the market district at the bottom, most of the public buildings and parks on the middle terrace, and private housing on the highest. The climate is humid subtropical, with significant rainfall throughout the year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ozurgeti was founded in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and is first mentioned by name in 1578 in the New Georgian Chronicles. It soon became the centre of Guria, an important place of trade and the home of the rulers of the Principality of Guria. A later find of a hoard of 270 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins under the town, the Ozurgeti Treasure, is regarded as evidence of this.</p><p>On 14 December 1846 it was designated a city, with just over 300 residents, and by 1865 the population had grown to over 700. It was a strategic place in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1877-1878">Russo-Turkish War</a> (1877–78), and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> it was the administrative centre of the Ozurgeti Uyezd of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutaisi-governorate">Kutaisi Governorate</a>. The fourth city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> to have a legal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press, it received one in 1891. A rail line connecting it to the rest of the country was established on 26 December 1923. On 9 July 1934 it was renamed Makharadze in honour of the Georgian Bolshevik Filipp Makharadze, and it took back its original name on 15 May 1989.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ozurgeti is the capital of the Guria region and the administrative centre of Ozurgeti District, and a regional centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> and hazelnut processing. The Ozurgeti Dramatic Theatre, founded in 1868, is named after Alexander Tsutsunava and occupies a five-storey Soviet Neoclassical building of 1962. The History Museum, founded in 1936, holds over 6,000 artifacts. Gurieli Palace, built in 1873, is now the seat of the Shemokmedi Orthodox Bishopric, and the ruins of a two-storey Byzantine-era bathhouse have been found in the centre of the town.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ozurgeti” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The princes ruled from <b>Ozurgeti</b> over a land of maize, vineyards and forested hills.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-guria">Principality of Guria</a></li></ul></section>
